<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claude McKay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Festus Claudius McKay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claude McKay was a Jamaican-American writer and poet and a central figure in the Harlem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1914, and in 1919 wrote the sonnet If We Must Die in response to the wave of white-on-black race riots and lynchings that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His poetry collection Harlem Shadows (1922) was among the first books of the Harlem Renaissance, and his novel Home to Harlem (1928) was a best-seller that won the Harmon Gold Award for Literature. A member of the Industrial Workers of the World and co-editor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberator</a>, he visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922–1923 but left, critical of the regime's authoritarian bent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claude-mckay.jpg" alt="Claude McKay" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James L. Allen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_McKay_James_L._Allen_Portrait_Edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1919 <b>Claude McKay</b> published his militant sonnet “If We Must Die”, in which Black readers heard defiance amid the race riots and lynchings of the time.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harlem Renaissance of the 1920s and 1930s, whose writers, among them Langston Hughes, <b>Claude McKay</b>, Alain Locke and W.E.B. Du Bois, addressed race relations and double consciousness, was a further influence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Background</a></li></ul></section>
